//   This extension adds the scountovf CSR, a 32-bit read-only register that contains shadow copies
//     of the OF bits in the 29 mhpmevent CSRs (mhpmevent3 - mhpmevent31) - where scountovf bit X
//     corresponds to mhpmeventX.
//
// Context:
//   scountovf is CSR 0xDA0, an S-level read-only address. Event-independent: OF bits are written by
//     software (issue 1981). OF of a counter that is not implemented may be read-only zero, so the
//     expected value is built from the OF bits read back from each mhpmevent. S-mode reads need
//     mcounteren bit X (scountovf_smode_read_access); mcounteren is WARL, so its read-back value is
//     used. The _Sm file covers M-mode accesses.
//
// Clarifications:
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/1981: software may write OF directly. gfavor:
//     "'Count overflow' and the OF bit are related but architecturally distinct. A count overflow
//     is a hardware event that results in setting of the OF bit by hardware. Hardware never clears
//     the OF bit. Software, on the other hand, can freely write 0 or 1 to the OF bit."
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/2287: with Smcdeleg/Ssccfg and menvcfg.CDE=1,
//     VS/VU reads of scountovf raise virtual-instruction; with CDE=0 "Smcdeleg effectively does not
//     exist" (gfavor). Tests clear menvcfg.CDE.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. OF patterns all 29 set, 0x55555550, 0xAAAAAAA8, none, and only the designated counter:
//     scountovf read in M-mode (T-SBI) equals the OF mask, and read in S-mode (mcounteren HPM bits
//     set) equals the OF mask AND mcounteren. Bits 2:0 are not checked; on RV64 bits 63:32 must be
//     0.
//   2. Read-only: in S-mode, csrrw (rd=x0 and not), csrrs/csrrc with rs1!=x0 (also holding 0), and
//     csrrwi/csrrsi/csrrci with uimm!=0 raise illegal-instruction (stval 0 or the instruction); the
//     value is unchanged. csrrs/csrrc with rs1=x0 and csrrsi/csrrci with uimm=0 read without a
//     trap.
//   3. U-mode reads raise illegal-instruction (S-level CSR), with mcounteren and scounteren all
//     ones.
//   4. With H (menvcfg.CDE cleared, hcounteren all ones): a VU-mode read raises virtual-instruction
//     (the read is allowed in VS-mode), and a VS-mode csrrw raises illegal-instruction.
//   mcounteren and scounteren are restored.
//
